Phase 1: Domain and Infrastructure Intelligence
Passive collection:
- Enumerate subdomains with `subfinder -all -recursive` where appropriate.
- Use PD Cloud/DNS intelligence for related domains and subdomain lookup when available.
- Map ASN, org, IP, and CIDR ownership using `asnmap`.
- Query DNS records for root and discovered hosts: A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, NS, TXT, SOA.
- Extract SPF, DMARC, and visible DKIM selectors when discoverable.
- Inventory CDN, load balancer, cloud provider, and third-party CNAME patterns.
- Record dangling-looking CNAMEs only as DNS observations. Do not attempt takeover or claim validation.

Phase 2: Live Host Discovery and Technology Profiling
Active only if authorized:
- Probe discovered subdomains with `httpx`.
- Capture URL, status code, title, content length, redirects, web server, technologies, CDN/WAF indicators, TLS metadata, headers, and favicon/hash where available.
- Use `tlsx` for certificate inventory and SAN coverage.
- Categorize hosts as: primary app, API, auth, docs, marketing, status, staging/dev, infra/admin, single-purpose service, third-party.

Phase 3: Port and Service Mapping
Active only if authorized:
- Build unique IP list from DNS and HTTP observations.
- Run fast port discovery with `naabu` on top 1000 ports.
- Run targeted `nmap -sV --version-light -Pn` only against IP:port pairs found by naabu.
- Save versions, banners, protocols, and inferred service purpose.
- Document non-HTTP services separately: SSH, FTP, SMTP, databases, queues, admin panels, debug/management ports.

Phase 4: Web Crawling and Endpoint Discovery
Active only if authorized:
- Select top live hosts by business significance and response richness.
- Run `katana` with JavaScript crawling/parsing enabled.
- Run `urlfinder` for passive historical URL enrichment.
- Extract routes into categories:
  - REST APIs
  - GraphQL
  - OpenAPI/Swagger/Redoc/docs
  - health/status/metrics/debug
  - auth/session/account routes
  - file upload/download/export
  - well-known URIs
  - static assets and JS bundles

Phase 5: Core Application Browser Deep Dive
For the top 3-5 significant public applications:
- Navigate landing, login, docs, dashboards, settings, pricing, signup, and public feature pages where accessible.
- Capture screenshots of key pages.
- Use browser session endpoint extraction after navigation.
- Use network traffic sitemap/search/get to catalog observed API calls, methods, paths, status codes, content types, and auth indicators.
- Document architecture: SPA vs server-rendered, frontend framework, auth model, navigation structure, tenant/account signals, payment/export/integration flows.
- Do not log in unless credentials are explicitly provided and authorized.

Phase 6: JavaScript and Client-Side Intelligence
- Inventory JS bundles from httpx/katana/browser observations.
- Analyze bundles for API base URLs, WebSocket URLs, internal hostnames, route names, feature flags, third-party integrations, auth/OAuth logic, and source map exposure.
- Save extracted JS findings separately from crawled URL findings.
- Treat hardcoded endpoints as observations requiring verification.

Phase 7: Security Configuration Baseline
- Inventory HTTP headers across live hosts: HSTS, CSP, X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, cache headers, CORS headers.
- Document CORS values and observed origin behavior only from normal requests.
- Inventory TLS certificate issuer, SANs, expiry, protocol/cipher information where available.
- Do not classify missing headers as vulnerabilities.

